{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A psalm, the dedication of the house.</b> Some say: David commanded that the singers perform this psalm at the dedication of the First Temple. Others say: at the dedication of the Second Temple or the Third, for the days of exile were likened to days of illness, as I shall explain. And what is closest to me: this psalm was composed at the dedication of his house, the house of cedars, for Scripture did not mention the house of HaShem; like it: \"the blind and the lame shall not come into the house\" (II Samuel 5:8), as I explained in its place. At that time David fell ill and recovered from his illness. Rabbi Moses said: David mourned when Nathan told him that he would not build the house for HaShem, and when he told him that Solomon his son would build the house, then he girded himself with joy instead of his mourning, for his son was like him, and the enemy would not reproach him. And he said: grief of the soul is likened to illness of the body and is even more severe, in the manner of: \"a man's spirit sustains his illness\" (Proverbs 18:14)."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:28:55.898680242Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}